//? Title: Pooling: From Mean to Max, Aliasing and Shift Consistency
//? Subtitle: Max, average, global and 3-D pooling checked by hand; power-mean, log-sum-exp and GeM pooling between mean and max; adaptive pooling; the exact distribution of max-pooled noise; the probability that a max-pool output survives a one-pixel shift; anti-aliased blur pooling and its frequency response; and shift consistency of a digits CNN, using datamunge's nn::max_pool2d, nn::avg_pool2d, nn::power_mean_pool2d, nn::logsumexp_pool2d, nn::gem_pool2d, nn::adaptive_avg_pool2d, nn::adaptive_max_pool2d and nn::blur_pool2d
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Pooling summarises each window of a feature map by one number. This report checks max, average, global and 3-D pooling against hand computations, and the routing of their gradients. It adds pooling between the two extremes: power means and log-sum-exp, which increase with their parameter from a mean to the maximum, and GeM with a learnable exponent. It also adds adaptive pooling to a fixed grid and anti-aliased blur pooling.
Three exact results are verified. Max-pooled Gaussian noise follows the order-statistic density \(n\varphi\Phi^{n-1}\). A \(k\times k\) max-pool output survives a one-pixel shift of i.i.d. input with probability \((k-1)/(k+1)\), confirmed for \(k=2\) to 6. The binomial pre-filter of blur pooling passes amplitude \(\cos^{k-1}(\omega/2)\), confirmed to \(10^{-15}\).
On a zone plate, plain subsampling aliases and max pooling saturates, while blur pooling removes what the coarse grid cannot hold. In a small digits CNN trained briefly on centred images, max pooling keeps its prediction under all nine one-pixel shifts for only 6–11% of the test images. Max pooling followed by a blur raises that to 46–52% at about the same centred accuracy.
)

\toc

> The problem

Convolutional networks shrink their feature maps with pooling. A max pool keeps the strongest response in each window, and an average pool keeps the mean. Both have no parameters, and both are meant to make the network tolerant of small translations. Three questions are worth answering exactly. What do the operations and their gradients compute? Is there a useful family between mean and max? And how much translation tolerance does pooling really buy?
The last question has a sampling-theory answer. Pooling with stride \(s\) is filtering followed by subsampling, and subsampling folds high frequencies onto low ones (Zhang, 2019; Azulay and Weiss, 2019).

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.2_reusable_layer_library/pooling.cpp`. That example printed max and average pooling of small images with VALID and SAME padding, followed the gradients, checked global and 3-D pooling against reductions and measured how many pooled outputs survive a one-pixel shift. All of that is kept. The new functions are in `datamunge/nn/pooling_variants.hpp`.

> The method

>> Max, average and their gradients

For a window \(w\) of \(n\) entries, max pooling returns \(\max_{i\in w}x_i\) and average pooling returns \(\frac1n\sum_{i\in w}x_i\). With SAME padding, border windows extend past the image. Max pooling ignores the padding, and average pooling divides by the number of real entries, not by \(n\). The gradient of max pooling is 1 at the arg-max of each window and 0 elsewhere, so it routes everything to one input. Average pooling spreads \(1/n\) to every entry. Global pooling is one window covering all of \(H\times W\). Adaptive pooling to an \(h_o\times w_o\) grid gives output row \(i\) the input rows \(\lfloor iH/h_o\rfloor\) to \(\lceil(i+1)H/h_o\rceil-1\), and likewise for columns.

>> Between mean and max

The power mean of non-negative entries,

$$
M_p=\Big(\frac1n\sum_{i\in w}x_i^p\Big)^{1/p},\qquad \frac{\partial M_p}{\partial x_i}=\frac{x_i^{p-1}}{n\,M_p^{p-1}},
$$
\alttext(M p equals the p-th root of one over n times the sum of x i to the p. Its derivative with respect to x i equals x i to the p minus 1, over n times M p to the p minus 1.)

is non-decreasing in \(p\) (Hardy, Littlewood and Pólya, 1934). It tends to the geometric mean as \(p\to0\), equals the arithmetic mean at \(p=1\) and tends to the maximum as \(p\to\infty\). Since the maximum is one of the \(n\) terms, \(\max_i x_i\,n^{-1/p}\le M_p\le\max_ix_i\). `nn::power_mean_pool2d` applies it to \(|x|\). `nn::gem_pool2d` is the global version with a clamp \(x\ge\epsilon\) and an exponent that may be learned (Radenović, Tolias and Chum, 2018).
Log-sum-exp pooling (Pinheiro and Collobert, 2015),

$$
L_\beta=\frac1\beta\log\Big(\frac1n\sum_{i\in w}e^{\beta x_i}\Big),\qquad \frac{\partial L_\beta}{\partial x_i}=\operatorname{softmax}(\beta x)_i,
$$
\alttext(L beta equals one over beta times the log of one over n times the sum of e to the beta x i. Its derivative with respect to x i is the softmax of beta x, entry i.)

works for any sign. It lies between the mean (its limit as \(\beta\to0\), by Jensen's inequality) and the maximum, and \(\max_ix_i-L_\beta\le(\log n)/\beta\). Its gradient sums to 1 like max pooling's, but every entry receives a share. `nn::logsumexp_pool2d` subtracts each window's maximum before exponentiating, so large \(\beta\) does not underflow.

>> Pooling noise

If the entries are i.i.d. \(\mathcal N(0,1)\), the average of \(n=k^2\) of them has standard deviation \(1/k\). The maximum has the order-statistic density (David and Nagaraja, 2003)

$$
f_{\max}(x)=n\,\varphi(x)\,\Phi(x)^{n-1},
$$
\alttext(The density of the maximum at x equals n times phi of x times capital Phi of x to the n minus 1.)

where \(\varphi\) and \(\Phi\) are the standard normal density and distribution function. Its mean is 1.029 for \(n=4\) and 1.485 for \(n=9\). Max pooling therefore shifts noise upwards and shrinks its spread more slowly than averaging does.

>> Survival under a one-pixel shift

Take a \(k\times k\) max pool with stride \(k\) and shift the input right by one pixel. An output cell compares the maximum over columns \(\{c,\dots,c+k-1\}\) with the maximum over \(\{c-1,\dots,c+k-2\}\). Both windows lie inside \(k+1\) columns, of which they share \(k-1\). If the largest of the \(k(k+1)\) entries is in a shared column, both maxima equal it. If not, only one window contains it, and the two maxima differ. For i.i.d. continuous inputs every position is equally likely to hold the largest entry, so

$$
P(\text{output unchanged})=\frac{k(k-1)}{k(k+1)}=\frac{k-1}{k+1},
$$
\alttext(The probability that the output is unchanged equals k times k minus 1, over k times k plus 1, which is k minus 1 over k plus 1.)

which is \(1/3\) for the usual \(2\times2\) pool. Larger windows survive shifts more often, but even \(k=6\) changes more than a quarter of its outputs.

>> Aliasing and blur pooling

Keeping one sample in every \(s\) can only represent frequencies \(|\omega|\le\pi/s\). Anything faster folds onto a slower frequency, and a one-pixel shift then changes the result in a way that is not a shift of the output. The classical remedy is a low-pass filter before subsampling. The filter \([1,1]/2\) has frequency response \(e^{-i\omega/2}\cos(\omega/2)\). Convolving it with itself \(k-1\) times gives the binomial filter of length \(k\), whose amplitude response is

$$
|H_k(\omega)|=\big|\cos(\omega/2)\big|^{k-1}.
$$
\alttext(The amplitude response of the binomial filter of length k equals the absolute value of cos of omega over 2, to the power k minus 1.)

A \(2\times2\) average pool with stride 2 is exactly the case \(k=2\), followed by subsampling. `nn::blur_pool2d` applies the separable binomial filter of length \(k\) with reflect padding, then subsamples (Zhang, 2019). Zhang's MaxBlurPool is a max pool with stride 1 followed by a blur pool with stride 2. It keeps the max's selectivity but samples a smoothed map.

> Simple examples

>> Hand checks, gradients, global pooling and the two families

The program pools a \(5\times5\) ramp with \(2\times2\) windows, uses adaptive pooling and blur pooling to make \(3\times3\) outputs, and differentiates \(L=\sum\mathrm{pool}(x)\) on a \(4\times4\) image with one large entry per window. It compares global and 3-D pooling with reductions and evaluates both families on the window \(\{0.2,0.5,0.9,2.0\}\). It then downsamples a \(128\times128\) zone plate \(\tfrac12+\tfrac12\cos(\pi r^2/256)\), whose local frequency \(\pi r/128\) grows with the radius, by a factor of 4 in four ways.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pooling_zoneplate.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Padding;
using nn::Tensor;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

void print_map(const std::string& label, const Tensor& t, int prec = 1) {
  std::print("{} [{} × {}]:", label, t.size(1), t.size(2));
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < t.size(1); ++i) {
    std::print("{}", i ? " /" : "");
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < t.size(2); ++j) std::print(" {:.{}f}", t.at({0, i, j, 0}), prec);
  }
  std::print("\n");
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // 1. 2 × 2 windows with stride 2 on a 5 × 5 image, VALID and SAME.
  const Tensor x = nn::reshape(nn::arange(1, 26, 1, F64), {1, 5, 5, 1});
  print_map("input (rows separated by /)", x, 0);
  print_map("max pool, VALID", nn::max_pool2d(x, 2, 2, Padding::Valid), 0);
  print_map("average pool, VALID", nn::avg_pool2d(x, 2, 2, Padding::Valid), 1);
  print_map("max pool, SAME", nn::max_pool2d(x, 2, 2, Padding::Same), 0);
  print_map("average pool, SAME", nn::avg_pool2d(x, 2, 2, Padding::Same), 1);
  print_map("adaptive average to 3 × 3", nn::adaptive_avg_pool2d(x, 3, 3), 2);
  print_map("adaptive max to 3 × 3", nn::adaptive_max_pool2d(x, 3, 3), 0);
  print_map("blur pool (filter [1, 2, 1] / 4), stride 2", nn::blur_pool2d(x, 2, 3), 2);

  // 2. Gradients of L = sum(pool(x)) on a 4 × 4 image with one large entry per window.
  Tensor g = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{9, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 8, 1, 1, 1, 1, 7, 1, 6, 1}, {1, 4, 4, 1});
  g.requires_grad_();
  print_map("\ninput", g, 0);
  print_map("dL/dx, max pool", nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::max_pool2d(g, 2, 2)), {g})[0], 0);
  print_map("dL/dx, average pool", nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::avg_pool2d(g, 2, 2)), {g})[0], 2);
  print_map("dL/dx, log-sum-exp pool, β = 1", nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::logsumexp_pool2d(g, 1.0, 2, 2)), {g})[0], 4);
  print_map("dL/dx, power-mean pool, p = 4", nn::grad(nn::sum(nn::power_mean_pool2d(g, 4.0, 2, 2)), {g})[0], 4);

  // 3. Global pooling against reductions, and one 3-D cell.
  nn::Generator gen(1);
  const Tensor v = nn::normal({2, 7, 5, 3}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  std::print("\nGlobal average pool vs mean over H, W: {:.1e}; global max pool vs max over H, W: {:.1e}; GeM with p = 1 vs global average (inputs made positive): {:.1e}.\n",
             nn::max(nn::abs(nn::global_avg_pool2d(v) - nn::mean(v, {1, 2}))).item(), nn::max(nn::abs(nn::global_max_pool2d(v) - nn::max(v, {1, 2}))).item(),
             nn::max(nn::abs(nn::gem_pool2d(nn::abs(v), 1.0) - nn::global_avg_pool2d(nn::abs(v)))).item());
  const Tensor vol = nn::normal({1, 8, 8, 8, 2}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  const Tensor cell = nn::slice(vol, {0, 2, 4, 6, 1}, {1, 2, 2, 2, 1});
  std::print("3-D pooling [1, 8, 8, 8, 2] → [1, 4, 4, 4, 2], cell (1, 2, 3), channel 1: max {:.4f} (hand {:.4f}), average {:.4f} (hand {:.4f}).\n",
             nn::max_pool3d(vol, 2, 2).at({0, 1, 2, 3, 1}), nn::max(cell).item(), nn::avg_pool3d(vol, 2, 2).at({0, 1, 2, 3, 1}), nn::mean(cell).item());

  // 4. From average to max: power mean and log-sum-exp of one window.
  const Tensor w = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{0.2, 0.5, 0.9, 2.0}, {1, 2, 2, 1});
  std::print("\nWindow {{0.2, 0.5, 0.9, 2.0}} (mean 0.9, max 2.0):\n\n| parameter | power mean, p | log-sum-exp, β |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (double t : {0.01, 1.0, 2.0, 4.0, 16.0, 64.0, 1000.0})
    std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", t, nn::power_mean_pool2d(w, t, 2, 2).item(), nn::logsumexp_pool2d(w, t, 2, 2).item());

  // 5. Figure: a zone plate cos(π r² / 128) on 128 × 128, downsampled by 4 four ways (two stride-2 stages).
  const std::int64_t N = 128;
  std::vector<double> zp(static_cast<std::size_t>(N * N));
  for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < N; ++i)
    for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < N; ++j) zp[i * N + j] = 0.5 + 0.5 * std::cos(M_PI * (double(i * i + j * j)) / (2.0 * N));
  const Tensor z = nn::from_vector(zp, {1, N, N, 1});
  auto twice = [](auto f, const Tensor& t) { return f(f(t)); };
  const Tensor sub = twice([](const Tensor& t) { return nn::strided_slice(t, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {1, t.size(1), t.size(2), 1}, {1, 2, 2, 1}); }, z);
  const Tensor mx = twice([](const Tensor& t) { return nn::max_pool2d(t, 2, 2); }, z);
  const Tensor av = twice([](const Tensor& t) { return nn::avg_pool2d(t, 2, 2); }, z);
  const Tensor bl = twice([](const Tensor& t) { return nn::blur_pool2d(t, 2, 5); }, z);
  // Frequency content: the zone plate's local frequency at radius r is π r / 128 rad/pixel; after downsampling by 4,
  // the new Nyquist limit π corresponds to π/4 at the original scale, i.e. r = 32.
  auto outer_energy = [&](const Tensor& t) {  // mean |value − 0.5| beyond the Nyquist radius (r > 32 original = 8 downsampled)
    double s = 0, cnt = 0;
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < t.size(1); ++i)
      for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < t.size(2); ++j)
        if (i * i + j * j > 12 * 12) s += std::abs(t.at({0, i, j, 0}) - 0.5), ++cnt;
    return s / cnt;
  };
  std::print("\nZone plate 128 × 128 → 32 × 32. Mean |value − 0.5| beyond radius 48 (original pixels), where the plate oscillates faster than the new grid can represent:\n"
             "subsampling {:.3f}, max pool {:.3f}, average pool {:.3f}, blur pool (filter 5) {:.3f}; the input itself is {:.3f} there.\n",
             outer_energy(sub), outer_energy(mx), outer_energy(av), outer_energy(bl), [&] {
               double s = 0, cnt = 0;
               for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < N; ++j) if (i * i + j * j > 48 * 48) s += std::abs(zp[i * N + j] - 0.5), ++cnt;
               return s / cnt; }());
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> grey;
  for (int k = 0; k < 64; ++k) grey.push_back({static_cast<std::uint8_t>(k * 255 / 63), static_cast<std::uint8_t>(k * 255 / 63), static_cast<std::uint8_t>(k * 255 / 63)});
  auto heat = [&](const Tensor& t, const std::string& title) {
    const std::int64_t n = t.size(1);
    std::vector<double> xs(n), ys(n);
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> zz(n, std::vector<double>(n));
    std::iota(xs.begin(), xs.end(), 0.0);
    std::iota(ys.begin(), ys.end(), 0.0);
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
      for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < n; ++j) zz[i][j] = t.at({0, n - 1 - j, i, 0});  // row 0 at the top
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.image(xs, ys, zz, grey, 0.0, 1.0).x_limits(-0.5, n - 0.5).y_limits(-0.5, n - 0.5).title(title).x_label("column").y_label("row (from the bottom)");
    fig.add(pl);
  };
  heat(sub, "Subsampling (keep every 4th pixel)");
  heat(mx, "Max pool 2 × 2, twice");
  heat(av, "Average pool 2 × 2, twice");
  heat(bl, "Blur pool (filter 5), twice");
  fig.size(1100, 1060).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/pooling_zoneplate.svg");
}
```
