\image(figures/pooling_zoneplate.svg)
// result_end

\caption(A 128 × 128 zone plate, whose rings get finer away from the top-left corner, downsampled to 32 × 32 by keeping every fourth pixel, by two 2 × 2 max pools, by two 2 × 2 average pools and by two blur pools with the binomial filter of length 5.)
\alttext(Top left: subsampling produces strong black and white patterns all over the image, including large false rings centred in the middle and at the corners. Top right: max pooling makes almost the whole image white, with a few grey cells near the top-left. Bottom left: average pooling keeps the first rings at the top-left and leaves faint grey checkerboard patterns elsewhere. Bottom right: blur pooling keeps the first rings at the top-left and is uniform grey elsewhere.)

Every value matches its hand computation. The SAME average at the right edge is \((5+10)/2=7.5\) and at the corner 25, because padding is not counted. The adaptive windows for 5 rows into 3 cells are rows 0–1, 1–3 and 3–4, which overlap, so the first adaptive average is \((1+2+6+7)/4=4\) and the second \((2+3+4+7+8+9)/6=5.5\). On this linear ramp the blur pool returns the same values, because a symmetric filter applied to a linear function returns the value at the window centre.
Global pooling, GeM with \(p=1\) and the 3-D cell all agree exactly with direct reductions.

The gradients show the three behaviours. Max pooling sends 1 to each window's largest entry and nothing elsewhere. Average pooling sends 0.25 everywhere. Log-sum-exp with \(\beta=1\) sends almost all of it (0.999, 0.997, 0.993 and 0.980) to the largest entry and the rest in proportion to \(e^{x}\). The power mean with \(p=4\) gives 0.707 to the largest entry and small amounts to the others. Its gradients do not sum to 1, because \(M_p\) is homogeneous of degree 1: \(\sum_ix_i\,\partial M_p/\partial x_i=M_p\).

In the table both families rise from their small-parameter limits to the maximum 2. The power mean starts at 0.6537 for \(p=0.01\), near the geometric mean 0.6514, and log-sum-exp starts at 0.9023 for \(\beta=0.01\), near the arithmetic mean 0.9. At 1000 both are within 0.003 of the maximum.

The zone plate makes aliasing visible. Beyond radius 48 (original pixels), the rings oscillate faster than the \(32\times32\) grid can represent. There, plain subsampling keeps a mean deviation from grey of 0.317, as large as the input's own 0.318, but as false patterns at the wrong scale. Max pooling saturates to white (0.484). Average pooling suppresses most of it (0.028) but leaves faint moiré, and blur pooling with \(k=5\) leaves nothing (0.000).

> Checking the theory

>> Noise, frequency response, shift survival and monotonicity

The program max- and average-pools four \(240\times240\) images of \(\mathcal N(0,1)\) noise with \(k=2,3,4\) and compares the moments with numerical integration of the order-statistic density. It filters cosine images \(\cos(\omega j)\) at 24 frequencies with average pooling (\(k=2\)) and blur pooling (\(k=3,5,7\)), at stride 1 so that the filter is measured alone, and fits the output amplitude by least squares. It counts unchanged max-pool outputs under a one-pixel shift of a \(120\times1200\) noise image for \(k=2\) to 6. Finally, it evaluates both families on a grid of 101 parameter values from 0.01 to 1000.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pooling_theory.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Padding;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

double phi(double x) { return std::exp(-0.5 * x * x) / std::sqrt(2 * M_PI); }
double Phi(double x) { return 0.5 * std::erfc(-x / std::sqrt(2.0)); }

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{234, 88, 12}, green{22, 163, 74}, purple{147, 51, 234}, grey{148, 163, 184}, black{30, 30, 30};
  // 1. Pooling i.i.d. N(0, 1) noise: the max of n = k² values has density n φ(x) Φ(x)^(n-1); the average has std 1/k.
  nn::Generator gen(1);
  const Tensor noise = nn::normal({4, 240, 240, 1}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  std::print("| window | n | max pool: sample mean | exact E[max] | sample std | exact std | average pool: sample std | 1/k |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> hist_x(2), hist_y(2), dens_x(2), dens_y(2);
  for (int k : {2, 3, 4}) {
    const int n = k * k;
    const auto m = nn::max_pool2d(noise, k, k).to_vector<double>();
    const auto a = nn::avg_pool2d(noise, k, k).to_vector<double>();
    auto moments = [](const std::vector<double>& v) {
      double s = 0, ss = 0;
      for (double e : v) s += e, ss += e * e;
      const double mu = s / v.size();
      return std::pair{mu, std::sqrt(ss / v.size() - mu * mu)};
    };
    // Exact moments of the max by the trapezoid rule on [-8, 8].
    double e1 = 0, e2 = 0;
    const double h = 1e-4;
    for (double x = -8; x <= 8; x += h) {
      const double f = n * phi(x) * std::pow(Phi(x), n - 1) * h;
      e1 += x * f, e2 += x * x * f;
    }
    const auto [mm, ms] = moments(m);
    const auto [am, as] = moments(a);
    std::print("| {} × {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", k, k, n, mm, e1, ms, std::sqrt(e2 - e1 * e1), as, 1.0 / k);
    if (k <= 3) {
      const int idx = k - 2;
      const double lo = -2.5, hi = 4.0, bw = 0.125;
      const int bins = static_cast<int>((hi - lo) / bw);
      std::vector<double> cnt(bins, 0.0);
      for (double e : m)
        if (e >= lo && e < hi) cnt[static_cast<int>((e - lo) / bw)] += 1;
      for (int b = 0; b < bins; ++b) hist_x[idx].push_back(lo + (b + 0.5) * bw), hist_y[idx].push_back(cnt[b] / (m.size() * bw));
      for (double x = lo; x <= hi; x += 0.02) dens_x[idx].push_back(x), dens_y[idx].push_back(n * phi(x) * std::pow(Phi(x), n - 1));
    }
  }

  // 2. Frequency response: an image cos(ω j) along the columns, filtered without subsampling (stride 1).
  //    Binomial filter of length k: |H(ω)| = |cos(ω/2)|^(k-1); a 2-wide average is k = 2.
  const std::int64_t W = 256;
  std::vector<double> omegas;
  for (int i = 1; i <= 24; ++i) omegas.push_back(M_PI * i / 24.0);
  const std::vector<int> ks{2, 3, 5, 7};
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> resp(ks.size());
  double worst = 0;
  for (double w : omegas) {
    std::vector<double> img(static_cast<std::size_t>(8 * W));
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < 8; ++i)
      for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < W; ++j) img[i * W + j] = std::cos(w * j);
    const Tensor t = nn::from_vector(img, {1, 8, W, 1});
    for (std::size_t q = 0; q < ks.size(); ++q) {
      const Tensor y = ks[q] == 2 ? nn::avg_pool2d(t, 2, 1) : nn::blur_pool2d(t, 1, ks[q]);
      // Least-squares amplitude of a cos + b sin at frequency ω over interior columns of row 3.
      double cc = 0, ss = 0, cs = 0, yc = 0, ys = 0;
      for (std::int64_t j = 16; j < y.size(2) - 16; ++j) {
        const double c = std::cos(w * j), s = std::sin(w * j), v = y.at({0, 3, j, 0});
        cc += c * c, ss += s * s, cs += c * s, yc += v * c, ys += v * s;
      }
      const double det = cc * ss - cs * cs;
      double amp;
      if (std::abs(det) < 1e-9 * cc * ss + 1e-300) amp = std::sqrt(yc * yc / (cc * cc));  // ω = π: sin vanishes on integers
      else {
        const double a = (yc * ss - ys * cs) / det, b = (ys * cc - yc * cs) / det;
        amp = std::hypot(a, b);
      }
      resp[q].push_back(amp);
      worst = std::max(worst, std::abs(amp - std::pow(std::abs(std::cos(w / 2)), ks[q] - 1)));
    }
  }
  std::print("\nFrequency response at 24 frequencies in (0, π]: max |measured amplitude − |cos(ω/2)|^(k−1)| = {:.1e} over k = 2 (average pool), 3, 5, 7.\n", worst);
  std::print("Amplitude passed at ω = 3π/4 (aliased by stride 2 to π/2): k = 2: {:.4f}, 3: {:.4f}, 5: {:.4f}, 7: {:.4f}; plain subsampling passes 1.\n", resp[0][17], resp[1][17], resp[2][17],
             resp[3][17]);

  // 3. Probability that a k × k, stride-k max-pool output is unchanged when the input shifts right by one pixel.
  //    The two windows share k − 1 of their k + 1 columns, so for i.i.d. continuous inputs P = (k − 1)/(k + 1).
  const Tensor big = nn::normal({1, 120, 1201, 1}, 0.0, 1.0, gen, F64);
  const Tensor base = nn::slice(big, {0, 0, 1, 0}, {1, 120, 1200, 1}), shifted = nn::slice(big, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {1, 120, 1200, 1});
  std::vector<double> kx, p_sim, p_thy;
  std::print("\n| window k | windows compared | unchanged fraction | (k − 1)/(k + 1) |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (int k = 2; k <= 6; ++k) {
    const auto a = nn::max_pool2d(base, k, k).to_vector<double>(), b = nn::max_pool2d(shifted, k, k).to_vector<double>();
    double same = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < a.size(); ++i) same += a[i] == b[i];
    kx.push_back(k), p_sim.push_back(same / a.size()), p_thy.push_back((k - 1.0) / (k + 1.0));
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", k, a.size(), p_sim.back(), p_thy.back());
  }

  // 4. The two families on one window, against p and β on a log scale.
  const Tensor w4 = nn::from_vector(std::vector<double>{0.2, 0.5, 0.9, 2.0}, {1, 2, 2, 1});
  std::vector<double> lt, pm, ls;
  double min_step_pm = 1e9, min_step_ls = 1e9;
  for (double e = -2; e <= 3.0001; e += 0.05) {
    const double t = std::pow(10.0, e);
    lt.push_back(e), pm.push_back(nn::power_mean_pool2d(w4, t, 2, 2).item()), ls.push_back(nn::logsumexp_pool2d(w4, t, 2, 2).item());
    if (pm.size() > 1) min_step_pm = std::min(min_step_pm, pm.back() - pm[pm.size() - 2]), min_step_ls = std::min(min_step_ls, ls.back() - ls[ls.size() - 2]);
  }
  const double geo = std::pow(0.2 * 0.5 * 0.9 * 2.0, 0.25);
  std::print("\nWindow {{0.2, 0.5, 0.9, 2.0}}, parameter from 0.01 to 1000: smallest step between neighbouring grid values {:.1e} (power mean), {:.1e} (log-sum-exp), so both increase.\n",
             min_step_pm, min_step_ls);
  std::print("Power mean at p = 0.01: {:.4f}, geometric mean {:.4f}; log-sum-exp at β = 0.01: {:.4f}, arithmetic mean 0.9000; both at 1000: {:.4f}, {:.4f}; max 2.\n", pm.front(), geo, ls.front(),
             pm.back(), ls.back());

  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2, 2);
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.points(hist_x[0], hist_y[0], "2 × 2 max pool", blue, 4).lines(dens_x[0], dens_y[0], "exact, n = 4", black, 1.5);
    pl.points(hist_x[1], hist_y[1], "3 × 3 max pool", orange, 4).lines(dens_x[1], dens_y[1], "exact, n = 9", grey, 1.5);
    pl.x_limits(-2.5, 4).y_limits(0, 1.0).title("Max-pooled N(0, 1) noise").x_label("pooled value").y_label("density");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    const std::vector<RGB> cols{blue, orange, green, purple};
    std::vector<double> ox;
    for (double w : omegas) ox.push_back(w / M_PI);
    for (std::size_t q = 0; q < ks.size(); ++q) {
      std::vector<double> cx, cy;
      for (double u = 0; u <= 1.0001; u += 0.01) cx.push_back(u), cy.push_back(std::pow(std::abs(std::cos(M_PI * u / 2)), ks[q] - 1));
      pl.lines(cx, cy, "", grey, 1.2).points(ox, resp[q], ks[q] == 2 ? "average 2 (k = 2)" : std::format("binomial k = {}", ks[q]), cols[q], 4.5);
    }
    pl.abline_v(0.5, black, 1).text(0.3, 1.12, "Nyquist after stride 2");
    pl.x_limits(0, 1).y_limits(0, 1.45).title("Frequency response of the pre-filter").x_label("frequency ω / π (cycles: ω / 2π per pixel)").y_label("amplitude passed");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::vector<double> tx, ty;
    for (double k = 1.8; k <= 6.2; k += 0.05) tx.push_back(k), ty.push_back((k - 1) / (k + 1));
    pl.lines(tx, ty, "(k − 1)/(k + 1)", grey, 2).points(kx, p_sim, "simulated", blue, 6);
    pl.x_limits(1.5, 6.5).y_limits(0, 1).title("Max pool output unchanged by a 1-pixel shift").x_label("window = stride k").y_label("fraction unchanged");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  {
    auto pl = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    pl.abline_h(2.0, black, 1).abline_h(0.9, grey, 1).abline_h(geo, grey, 1);
    pl.lines(lt, pm, "power mean (p)", blue, 2.2).lines(lt, ls, "log-sum-exp (β)", orange, 2.2);
    pl.text(-1.3, 2.07, "max").text(2.2, 0.83, "arithmetic mean").text(2.2, geo - 0.07, "geometric mean");
    pl.x_limits(-2, 3).y_limits(0.4, 2.5).title("From mean to max on one window").x_label("log10 of p or β").y_label("pooled value");
    fig.add(pl);
  }
  fig.size(1100, 1000).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/pooling_theory.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| window | n | max pool: sample mean | exact E[max] | sample std | exact std | average pool: sample std | 1/k |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 × 2 | 4 | 1.0323 | 1.0294 | 0.7031 | 0.7012 | 0.4990 | 0.5000 |
| 3 × 3 | 9 | 1.4896 | 1.4850 | 0.6016 | 0.5978 | 0.3327 | 0.3333 |
| 4 × 4 | 16 | 1.7712 | 1.7660 | 0.5501 | 0.5431 | 0.2499 | 0.2500 |

Frequency response at 24 frequencies in (0, π]: max |measured amplitude − |cos(ω/2)|^(k−1)| = 1.4e-15 over k = 2 (average pool), 3, 5, 7.
Amplitude passed at ω = 3π/4 (aliased by stride 2 to π/2): k = 2: 0.3827, 3: 0.1464, 5: 0.0214, 7: 0.0031; plain subsampling passes 1.

| window k | windows compared | unchanged fraction | (k − 1)/(k + 1) |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 36000 | 0.3321 | 0.3333 |
| 3 | 16000 | 0.5014 | 0.5000 |
| 4 | 9000 | 0.5966 | 0.6000 |
| 5 | 5760 | 0.6606 | 0.6667 |
| 6 | 4000 | 0.7173 | 0.7143 |

Window {0.2, 0.5, 0.9, 2.0}, parameter from 0.01 to 1000: smallest step between neighbouring grid values 2.8e-04 (power mean), 1.7e-04 (log-sum-exp), so both increase.
Power mean at p = 0.01: 0.6537, geometric mean 0.6514; log-sum-exp at β = 0.01: 0.9023, arithmetic mean 0.9000; both at 1000: 1.9972, 1.9986; max 2.
\image(figures/pooling_theory.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Top left: histograms of 2 × 2 and 3 × 3 max-pooled N(0, 1) noise (points) and the exact densities (lines). Top right: measured amplitude of the filtered cosine (points) against the cosine-power curves (grey); the vertical line marks the Nyquist frequency of a stride-2 grid. Bottom left: fraction of max-pool outputs unchanged by a one-pixel shift against the window size, with the curve (k − 1)/(k + 1). Bottom right: power mean and log-sum-exp of one window against the logarithm of their parameter, with the maximum, the arithmetic mean and the geometric mean.)
\alttext(Top left: blue points follow the black curve, which peaks at about 0.57 near 1; orange points follow the grey curve, which peaks at about 0.68 near 1.4. Top right: four rows of points fall from 1 at low frequency to 0 at frequency pi, each exactly on its grey curve; the more taps, the faster the fall; at the Nyquist line the amplitudes are about 0.71, 0.5, 0.25 and 0.13. Bottom left: five points rise from 0.33 at k = 2 to 0.72 at k = 6, on the grey curve. Bottom right: the orange curve rises from 0.9 to 2 and the blue curve from 0.65 to 2, both S-shaped, with the orange one rising earlier.)

The max-pooled noise matches the exact density in the figure. The sample means 1.032, 1.490 and 1.771 lie within 1.3 standard errors of the exact 1.029, 1.485 and 1.766. The standard deviations 0.703, 0.602 and 0.550 compare with the exact 0.701, 0.598 and 0.543. Average pooling gives 0.499, 0.333 and 0.250 against \(1/k\).

The measured filter amplitudes agree with \(|\cos(\omega/2)|^{k-1}\) to \(1.4\times10^{-15}\) at all 24 frequencies and all four filters. The frequency \(3\pi/4\) is the critical case: a stride-2 grid folds it onto \(\pi/2\). There the average pool passes 0.383 of the amplitude, and the binomial filters of length 3, 5 and 7 pass 0.146, 0.021 and 0.003, while plain subsampling passes all of it.

The shift-survival fractions are 0.332, 0.501, 0.597, 0.661 and 0.717 for \(k=2\) to 6, against \((k-1)/(k+1)=0.333\), 0.500, 0.600, 0.667 and 0.714.

Both families increase across the grid: the smallest step between neighbouring parameter values is \(+2.8\times10^{-4}\) for the power mean and \(+1.7\times10^{-4}\) for log-sum-exp. On this window log-sum-exp lies above the power mean at every parameter value.

> A dataset: shift consistency of a digits CNN

The digits data (Alpaydin and Kaynak, 1998) has 1797 images of \(8\times8\) pixels. Each is placed on a \(12\times12\) canvas at offset (2, 2) for training. After a fixed shuffle, 1437 images train and 360 test. The test images are also placed at the nine offsets \((d_y,d_x)\in\{1,2,3\}^2\), which are one-pixel shifts around the training position.
The network is a \(3\times3\) convolution with 8 channels, ReLU and a pool, then a \(3\times3\) convolution with 16 channels, ReLU and a pool, then global average pooling and a dense layer with 10 outputs. Both pools use the same method. The last variant uses max pooling with a GeM head (learned \(p\), starting at 3) in place of global average pooling. Adam (step \(10^{-2}\)) runs for 500 steps with batches of 32 and no augmentation, for two seeds. This short schedule keeps the run to a few seconds; the networks are not trained to convergence, so the centred accuracies are a few points below what longer training reaches. Consistency is the share of test images that get the same prediction at all nine offsets.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/pooling_digits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
namespace init = dm::nn::init;
using nn::Activation;
using nn::Padding;
using nn::Tensor;
using dm::graphics::RGB;
const auto F64 = nn::DType::Float64;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  // Digits placed on a 12 × 12 canvas: centred (offset 2, 2) for training; offsets 1-3 for the shift tests.
  const auto digits = dm::datasets::digits();
  const std::int64_t n = 1797, C = 12;
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> pix(64);
  for (int j = 0; j < 64; ++j) pix[j] = digits.get_column<double>(std::format("pixel_{}", j)).values();
  const auto labels = digits.get_column<double>("digit").values();
  auto canvas = [&](const std::vector<std::int64_t>& rows, std::int64_t dy, std::int64_t dx) {
    std::vector<double> v(rows.size() * C * C, 0.0);
    for (std::size_t r = 0; r < rows.size(); ++r)
      for (int a = 0; a < 8; ++a)
        for (int b = 0; b < 8; ++b) v[(r * C + a + dy) * C + b + dx] = pix[a * 8 + b][rows[r]] / 16.0;
    return nn::from_vector(v, {static_cast<std::int64_t>(rows.size()), C, C, 1});
  };
  std::vector<std::int64_t> perm(n);
  std::iota(perm.begin(), perm.end(), 0);
  std::mt19937_64 srng(0);
  std::shuffle(perm.begin(), perm.end(), srng);
  const std::int64_t n_test = 360, n_train = n - n_test;
  const std::vector<std::int64_t> tr(perm.begin(), perm.begin() + n_train), te(perm.begin() + n_train, perm.end());
  const Tensor Xtr = canvas(tr, 2, 2);
  std::vector<double> ytr_v, yte_v;
  for (auto i : tr) ytr_v.push_back(labels[i]);
  for (auto i : te) yte_v.push_back(labels[i]);
  const Tensor ytr = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(ytr_v, {n_train}), nn::DType::Int64), yte = nn::cast(nn::from_vector(yte_v, {n_test}), nn::DType::Int64);
  std::vector<Tensor> Xte_shift;  // 9 offsets, (dy, dx) in {1, 2, 3}²
  for (std::int64_t dy = 1; dy <= 3; ++dy)
    for (std::int64_t dx = 1; dx <= 3; ++dx) Xte_shift.push_back(canvas(te, dy, dx));

  const std::vector<std::string> names{"max", "average", "blur (k = 3)", "max then blur", "log-sum-exp, β = 4", "power mean, p = 3", "subsampling", "max + GeM head"};
  const int steps = 500;
  std::print("| pooling (both stages) | test accuracy, centred % | mean accuracy over 9 shifts % | consistent over all 9 shifts % | shift by 1 changes the prediction % |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<std::vector<double>> acc_c(names.size()), cons(names.size());
  std::vector<double> gem_p;
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < names.size(); ++m) {
    std::vector<double> a0, a9, c9, flip;
    for (std::uint64_t seed : {1, 2}) {
      nn::ParameterStore p(seed, F64);
      auto pool = [&](const Tensor& h) {
        switch (m) {
          case 0: case 7: return nn::max_pool2d(h, 2, 2);
          case 1: return nn::avg_pool2d(h, 2, 2);
          case 2: return nn::blur_pool2d(h, 2, 3);
          case 3: return nn::blur_pool2d(nn::max_pool2d(h, 2, 1, Padding::Same), 2, 3);
          case 4: return nn::logsumexp_pool2d(h, 4.0, 2, 2);
          case 5: return nn::power_mean_pool2d(h, 3.0, 2, 2);
          default: return nn::strided_slice(h, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {h.size(0), h.size(1), h.size(2), h.size(3)}, {1, 2, 2, 1});
        }
      };
      auto f = [&](const Tensor& x) {
        const nn::ConvLayerOptions co{.activation = Activation::Relu, .init = init::he_normal()};
        Tensor h = pool(nn::conv2d(p, "c1", x, 8, 3, co));
        h = pool(nn::conv2d(p, "c2", h, 16, 3, co));
        const Tensor g = m == 7 ? nn::gem_pool2d(h, p.get("gem/p", {1}, init::constant(3.0)), 1e-6) : nn::global_avg_pool2d(h);
        return nn::dense(p, "out", g, 10);
      };
      (void)f(nn::slice(Xtr, {0, 0, 0, 0}, {2, C, C, 1}));
      nn::Optimizer opt(p, 1e-2, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
      std::mt19937_64 rng(seed);
      std::uniform_int_distribution<std::int64_t> pick(0, n_train - 1);
      for (int s = 0; s < steps; ++s) {
        std::vector<std::int64_t> idx(32);
        for (auto& i : idx) i = pick(rng);
        const Tensor ix = nn::from_vector(idx, {32});
        opt.minimize(nn::sparse_softmax_cross_entropy(f(nn::gather(Xtr, ix, 0)), nn::gather(ytr, ix, 0)));
      }
      nn::NoGradGuard ng;
      std::vector<std::vector<double>> pred(9);
      double acc_sum = 0;
      for (int k = 0; k < 9; ++k) {
        pred[k] = nn::argmax(f(Xte_shift[k]), 1).to_vector<double>();
        double c = 0;
        for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n_test; ++i) c += pred[k][i] == yte_v[i];
        acc_sum += c / n_test;
        if (k == 4) a0.push_back(100 * c / n_test);
      }
      double consistent = 0, changed = 0, pairs = 0;
      for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n_test; ++i) {
        bool all = true;
        for (int k = 0; k < 9; ++k) all = all && pred[k][i] == pred[4][i];
        consistent += all;
        for (int k : {1, 3, 5, 7}) changed += pred[k][i] != pred[4][i], ++pairs;  // the 4 one-pixel neighbours of the centre
      }
      a9.push_back(100 * acc_sum / 9), c9.push_back(100 * consistent / n_test), flip.push_back(100 * changed / pairs);
      if (m == 7) gem_p.push_back(p.at("gem/p").at({0}));
    }
    acc_c[m] = a0, cons[m] = c9;
    std::print("| {} | {:.1f}, {:.1f} | {:.1f}, {:.1f} | {:.1f}, {:.1f} | {:.1f}, {:.1f} |\n", names[m], a0[0], a0[1], a9[0], a9[1], c9[0], c9[1], flip[0], flip[1]);
  }
  std::print("\nLearned GeM exponent after training (started at 3): {:.2f}, {:.2f}.\n", gem_p[0], gem_p[1]);

  auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<RGB> cols{{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {234, 88, 12}, {147, 51, 234}, {219, 39, 119}, {14, 165, 233}, {148, 163, 184}, {30, 30, 30}};
  for (std::size_t m = 0; m < names.size(); ++m) plot.points(cons[m], acc_c[m], names[m], cols[m], 7);
  plot.x_limits(-3, 103).y_limits(80, 100).title("Digits CNN: accuracy against consistency under shifts (each seed)").x_label("test images with the same prediction at all 9 shifts (%)")
      .y_label("test accuracy, centred (%)");
  plot.size(1100, 480).save("build/pooling_digits.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| pooling (both stages) | test accuracy, centred % | mean accuracy over 9 shifts % | consistent over all 9 shifts % | shift by 1 changes the prediction % |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| max | 93.3, 94.4 | 52.9, 60.1 | 6.1, 11.1 | 35.1, 31.8 |
| average | 88.6, 87.2 | 61.0, 68.1 | 18.3, 29.2 | 26.9, 21.0 |
| blur (k = 3) | 85.8, 83.1 | 58.6, 71.9 | 16.4, 42.2 | 29.2, 18.1 |
| max then blur | 91.7, 93.6 | 79.2, 83.7 | 46.4, 51.9 | 12.8, 9.2 |
| log-sum-exp, β = 4 | 92.8, 92.5 | 54.9, 56.1 | 13.9, 13.9 | 35.1, 30.4 |
| power mean, p = 3 | 93.6, 91.7 | 57.2, 66.6 | 7.5, 23.3 | 31.1, 24.2 |
| subsampling | 91.7, 93.6 | 33.3, 32.6 | 0.0, 0.3 | 64.0, 64.0 |
| max + GeM head | 95.8, 96.9 | 58.5, 61.5 | 14.4, 22.8 | 34.4, 25.4 |

Learned GeM exponent after training (started at 3): 4.98, 5.17.
\image(figures/pooling_digits.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Centred test accuracy against the share of test images whose prediction is the same at all nine one-pixel offsets, for each pooling method and seed.)
\alttext(The grey subsampling points are at the far left, near 0 percent consistency and 92 to 94 percent accuracy. Max, log-sum-exp and power-mean points cluster at 6 to 23 percent consistency and 92 to 94 percent accuracy; the GeM points are at 14 and 23 percent consistency and 96 to 97 percent accuracy. The orange blur points are at 16 and 42 percent consistency and 83 to 86 percent accuracy. The green average-pool points are at 18 and 29 percent consistency and about 87 to 89 percent accuracy. The purple max-then-blur points are at 46 and 52 percent consistency and 92 to 94 percent accuracy, to the right of all others.)

On the centred test images the methods reach 83.1% to 96.9% after this short schedule. The max-like methods and subsampling are at 91.7% to 94.4%, the GeM head is highest (95.8% and 96.9%), and average and blur pooling, which learn more slowly, are lowest (83.1% to 88.6%). Shifting by one pixel separates them. Plain subsampling, which is what a strided layer without a filter does, changes 64% of the predictions. Only 0% and 0.3% of the test images keep their prediction at all nine offsets, and the mean accuracy over the offsets falls to 33%. Max pooling changes 32–35% of the predictions and keeps 6–11% of the images consistent.
Log-sum-exp (\(\beta=4\)), power-mean pooling (\(p=3\)) and the GeM head behave like max pooling, with 8–23% consistency. The learned GeM exponent moved from 3 to 4.98 and 5.17, towards max pooling.

Average pooling is more consistent than max pooling (18% and 29%), at a cost in accuracy (88.6% and 87.2%). A blur pool alone is erratic: 16% and 42% consistency with the two seeds, at 85.8% and 83.1% accuracy. Max pooling followed by a blur, Zhang's MaxBlurPool, is the clear winner. It keeps 46% and 52% of the images consistent and changes only 9.2–12.8% of the predictions under a one-pixel shift. Its mean accuracy over the nine offsets is 79–84%, against 53–60% for max pooling, with centred accuracy (91.7% and 93.6%) close to max pooling's.
With only two seeds, 360 test images and short training, differences of a few points between the max-like methods are within noise. The gaps to subsampling and MaxBlurPool are not.

> Conclusion

Max and average pooling and their SAME, global, adaptive and 3-D forms all match hand computations exactly. Their gradients differ in kind: max routes everything to one entry, average spreads it evenly. Power-mean and log-sum-exp pooling fill the space between with a single increasing parameter, and their gradients are smooth versions of max pooling's. The power mean starts from the geometric mean, log-sum-exp from the arithmetic mean.
The new power-mean function guards against the zero windows that ReLU produces. Without the guard they make the derivative of \(t^{1/p}\) infinite, and training produced NaN gradients and stayed at chance.

Pooling's translation tolerance is limited and predictable. A \(2\times2\) max pool keeps only a third of its outputs under a one-pixel shift of random input, exactly as \((k-1)/(k+1)\) says. Plain subsampling aliases, and the binomial pre-filter suppresses the folded frequencies by \(\cos^{k-1}(\omega/2)\), exactly as derived.
In a small CNN trained briefly without augmentation, this is the difference between a prediction that changes under a one-pixel shift for 64% of the images (subsampling), about a third (max pooling) and about a tenth (max pooling followed by a blur), at little cost in accuracy on centred images.

